/*
 * The roster of MIR nodes - one row per `mir::Node::Kind`, and the only place a kind is written
 * down. The enum, the concrete-type cast in `visitNode`, the mnemonic and the traits are all
 * generated from it, so a kind cannot be added without deciding every column for it.
 *
 * The difference from the current resolver's inst.def is what a missing answer does.
 * `Analysis-IR.md` §1.3 is about ownership effects defaulting to *absence*, which is the unsafe
 * direction: an instruction that forgot to declare a transfer looked exactly like an ordinary read.
 * Here there is no ownership column at all, because ownership is not a property of a kind - it is a
 * `UseMode` on each operand, supplied when the node is built and refused by the builder if it is
 * missing. What is left in this table is structure, and `verifyBody` checks that a node's declared
 * structure and its stored fields agree.
 *
 * ## The columns
 *
 *  - `kind` is the `mir::Node::Kind` enumerator, and the order of these rows is the order of the enum.
 *  - `Struct` is the concrete type a `Node` of that kind may be cast to; `visitNode` is generated
 *    from this column, so several kinds may share one struct where they are one node with two
 *    meanings.
 *  - `mnemonic` is what a dump prints for the kind. A node that refines it from its own fields does
 *    so in print.cpp; what is here is the base name.
 *  - `flags` is what a pass may ask about a *kind* rather than about a node:
 *      - `kMirResult` - defines a value another node may name by ID, and is what licenses the cast
 *        to `NodeResult` that reaches its occurrence. Its absence is checkable and structural: a
 *        terminator or a store is not an operand source, does not derive from `NodeResult`, and
 *        therefore does not carry twelve bytes of result type it has no result for.
 *      - `kMirTerminator` - ends its block, and is the only kind that may name successors.
 *
 * There were three columns and there are two. The one that went was `kMirSuspends`, and it is worth
 * recording why rather than quietly dropping it: whether a call may suspend the enclosing fiber is a
 * property of the *callee's published summary* (Analysis-Sharing.md §3.3), not of the fact that the
 * node is a call. A kind-level bit can answer only "every call suspends" or "no call does", both of
 * which are wrong, and the across-suspend rule needs the per-call answer to have anything to check
 * live loans against. It is `Summary::suspension` on the selected callee, read through the call node.
 *
 * ## `GenValue` is not a constant
 *
 * `fn (n: I32) width() -> I32 = n` reads a number the body will not know until it is instantiated,
 * so the node names the **parameter** and never a value. Under a specialization the conversion
 * substitutes it and finds a `ConstType`, which is where it becomes a literal; in the erased form
 * it is a load from a const slot. Folding it into `Const` would have meant a constant that is not
 * one, which is the state every reader of `Body::constants` would then have to test for.
 *
 * ## `Argument` is a node
 *
 * A block's parameters are `Argument` nodes held in `Block::arguments` rather than a second list of
 * bare occurrences, so an operand naming one names it the way it names any other value - by node ID,
 * through `SourceKind::Result`. The alternative was a fourth source kind meaning "argument N of the
 * block this operand is written in", and it is worse in exactly the way this representation is
 * meant to avoid: an operand whose meaning depends on where it is standing. They are the one node
 * kind that is not in a block's `nodes` list, because they have no position among the instructions -
 * they hold on entry.
 *
 * ## The mnemonic is a base name
 *
 * `binary`, `unary` and `write` each print something narrower than their row says - `add`, `neg`,
 * `write.init` - and print.cpp is where that happens. What is in this column is what the kind is
 * called when nothing narrower is known, which is what a verifier's diagnostic wants.
 */

//            kind          struct            mnemonic          flags
YANA_MIR_INST( Argument,     NodeArgument,     "argument"_v,     kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Const,        NodeConst,        "const"_v,        kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( GenValue,     NodeGenValue,     "gen.value"_v,    kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Read,         NodeRead,         "read"_v,         kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Binary,       NodeBinary,       "binary"_v,       kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Unary,        NodeUnary,        "unary"_v,        kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Aggregate,    NodeAggregate,    "aggregate"_v,    kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Call,         NodeCall,         "call"_v,         kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Write,        NodeWrite,        "write"_v,        0 )
YANA_MIR_INST( Return,       NodeReturn,       "return"_v,       kMirTerminator )
YANA_MIR_INST( Jump,         NodeJump,         "jump"_v,         kMirTerminator )
YANA_MIR_INST( Yield,        NodeYield,        "yield"_v,        kMirTerminator )
YANA_MIR_INST( Branch,       NodeBranch,       "branch"_v,       kMirTerminator )
YANA_MIR_INST( Unreachable,  NodeUnreachable,  "unreachable"_v,  kMirTerminator )
